<resources>
    <string name="app_name">NutriLens AI</string>
    <!-- Filled in from the training/evaluation run; see docs/MODEL_CARD.md -->
    <string name="about_model">A MobileNetV3 image model runs on your phone, with no internet needed. It knows 122 dishes: the 101 of the Food-101 research dataset (mostly international dishes) plus 21 Indian dishes such as biryani, chapati, dal, idli, masala dosa, pav bhaji and vada pav.</string>
    <string name="about_accuracy">Accuracy on held-out photos: being measured.</string>
    <!-- UI text moved out of layouts -->
    <string name="cd_back">Back</string>
    <string name="about_data_sources">About &amp; data sources</string>
    <string name="how_recognition_works">How recognition works</string>
    <string name="it_recognises_one_dish_per_photo_mark">It recognises one dish per photo (mark several foods on one plate yourself) and shows its next guesses, so you can correct it in one tap. Confidence is calibrated, but a model can still be confidently wrong. When you correct it, NutriLens remembers that photo (as numbers, not the picture) and recognises the same dish better next time. A photo-quality check (OpenCV) warns about blurry, dark or glaring photos and brightens dark ones before recognition.</string>
    <string name="see_all_supported_foods">See all supported foods</string>
    <string name="where_the_nutrition_numbers_come_from">Where the nutrition numbers come from</string>
    <string name="usda_fooddata_central_food_and_nutrien">USDA FoodData Central, Food and Nutrient Database for Dietary Studies (FNDDS 2021–2023), public domain. Each food uses USDA\'s values per 100 g and a typical portion. Dishes USDA lists only as ingredients (for example pav bhaji = vegetable curry + butter + 2 pav) are added up from those ingredients. Every food shows its exact source on its detail screen. Packaged foods scanned by barcode come from Open Food Facts (open data, ODbL licence), entered by volunteers from pack labels.</string>
    <string name="exceptions_dhokla_has_no_usda_entry_an">Exceptions: 42 more Indian dishes (paneer tikka, palak paneer, poha, sambar and others) can be found and logged by hand, but not recognised by the camera. Their values are typical-recipe estimates, not lab measurements; each says so on its detail screen. Dhokla is also a recipe estimate. In kadai paneer, paneer is counted as whole-milk mozzarella because the USDA paneer entry looks sweetened. % Daily Value uses FDA reference values for a 2,000 kcal diet.</string>
    <string name="good_to_know">Good to know</string>
    <string name="portions_are_not_measured_from_the_pho">• Portions are not measured from the photo. Pick how much you ate.\n• Restaurant and home recipes vary; values are typical, not exact.\n• The Nutrition Score is an educational estimate, not medical or dietary advice.\n• Photos, meals and your profile stay on this phone. The only thing that goes online is a barcode number, when you look up a packaged food in Open Food Facts.</string>
    <string name="training_photos_food_101_bossard_et_al">Training photos: Food-101 (Bossard et al., ETH Zurich, 2014) and two community Indian food datasets from Hugging Face: one mirrored from Kaggle (web images, no license stated) and one released as CC0. The dish photos shown in the app are one example from these datasets per dish (CC0 set first). Fonts: Plus Jakarta Sans (SIL Open Font License).</string>
